/-!
Hofer–Wysocki–Zehnder, *Polyfold and Fredholm Theory* (Springer 2021; 2017 arXiv 1707.08941),
Part II, Chapter 10, §10.5 "Localization in the Strong Bundle Case" (pp. 504–506; 2017:
pp. 364–366): the construction before Definition 10.5.1, **Definitions 10.5.1, 10.5.2 and
Remark 10.5.3** (2017: Definitions 10.14, 10.15; Remark 10.5.3 is new in the 2021 edition).
Numbers and pages are the 2021 text's, the 2017 ones in parentheses.  Theorem 10.5.4, the
positive half of Remark 10.5.5, Proposition 10.5.6 and Theorem 10.5.7 are treated in
`StrongBundleLocalizationSections.lean`; the witnesses and the negative half of Remark 10.5.5 are
in `StrongBundleLocalizationExamples.lean`.

# Diagrams of strong bundles and generalized strong bundle maps

## The text, paraphrased

(pp. 504–505) For strong bundles `(P : W → X, μ)`, `(P' : W' → X', μ')` over ep-groupoids one
considers diagrams `D : W ←Φ W'' →Ψ W'` with `Φ` a strong bundle equivalence and `Ψ` a strong
bundle functor; refinements and equivalence (having a common refinement) are introduced as for
ep-groupoids, and the class `[D] : W → W'` is a generalized strong bundle map.  `D` covers a
diagram `d : X ←φ X'' →ψ X'`, so `[D]` covers `[d]`; `|[D]| = |Ψ| ∘ |Φ|⁻¹ : |W| → |W'|` does not
depend on the representative.  The symbolic diagram (10.24) becomes, on orbit spaces, the
commutative square `|P'| ∘ |[D]| = |[d]| ∘ |P|`.  For `|[d]|(|x|) = |x'|` there are `x''` and
`a : φ(x'') → x`, `b : ψ(x'') → x'`, defining the homomorphism `γ : G_x → G'_{x'}`, the
composite of `g ↦ a⁻¹ ∘ g ∘ a`, `φ⁻¹`, `ψ` and `ℓ ↦ b ∘ ℓ ∘ b⁻¹`; an equivariant germ
`q : O(X, x) → O(X', x')` with `q(g ∗ z) = γ(g) ∗ q(z)`, and its lift `Q` (10.25).
(Definition 10.5.1, p. 506) `SEP(F⁻¹)` has the strong bundles over ep-groupoids as objects and the
generalized strong bundle maps as morphisms.  (Definition 10.5.2) A generalized strong bundle
isomorphism is a class `[D]` of diagrams in which both `Φ` and `Ψ` are strong bundle
equivalences.  (Remark 10.5.3) For ep-groupoids this form was a theorem (Theorem 10.3.10); for
strong bundles it is built into the definition.

## What is formalized (namespace `Polyfold.SBLocalization`)

* Helpers: `StrongBundleNatTrans.toEpNatTrans`, `ofEpNatTrans`, `symm`, `trans`, `whiskerLeft`;
  `SEP.NatEquiv` (Definition 10.4.8 for morphisms of `SEP`) with `refl`, `symm`, `trans`,
  `comp_left`, `orbitMap_eq`, `total`, `base`; `SEP.baseEP`, `SEP.totalEP` (`X` and `(W, 𝐖)` as
  objects of `EP`).
* `SDiagram A A'` (the diagrams); `SDiagram.IsRefinement` with the examples
  `isRefinement_precomp` and `isRefinement_of_natEquiv`; `IsRefinement.refl`,
  `IsRefinement.trans` (Lemma 10.3.2 for strong bundles); `IsCommonRefinement`, `Equiv`
  (`refl`, `symm`).
* `SDiagram.base` (`d`), `SDiagram.total`, `IsRefinement.base`, `IsRefinement.total`,
  `Equiv.base`, `Equiv.total`; `SDiagram.orbitMap` (`|D|`), `IsRefinement.orbitMap_eq`,
  `Equiv.orbitMap_eq`, `SDiagram.orbitMap_square` (the diagram `|D|`).
* `EPLocalization.Diagram.isotropyHom` (`γ`), `isotropyHom_spec`, `bijective_isotropyHom`,
  `exists_isotropyData`.
* `GenSBMap A A'` (the generalized strong bundle maps), `SDiagram.cls` (`[D]`),
  `GenSBMap.base` (`[d]`), `GenSBMap.total`, `GenSBMap.orbitMap` (`|[D]|`),
  `GenSBMap.orbitMap_square`.  **Definition 10.5.1**: `SEPLoc` with its `Quiver` instance,
  `SEPLoc.idHom`, `SEPLoc.localizationPrefunctor`.  **Definition 10.5.2**:
  `GenSBMap.IsGeneralizedIso`.  **Remark 10.5.3**: `GenSBMap.remark_10_5_3`; `SDiagram.symm`,
  `isGeneralizedIso_symm`, `isGeneralizedIso_ofHom`.

## What the text leaves implicit, and the choices made

* Refinements use the natural equivalence of strong bundle functors (Definition 10.4.8), the
  refining functor being a strong bundle equivalence; this is our reading of "following our
  earlier construction".
* **The classes.**  The transitivity of "having a common refinement" is the strong bundle version
  of Lemma 10.3.4, whose proof uses the weak fibred product of strong bundles over ep-groupoids.
  The text does not construct it and the repository does not have it (not even the pull-back of a
  strong bundle over an ep-groupoid along a functor, see `Chapter8/Book2021.lean`).  The classes
  `[D]` are therefore taken in `Quot SDiagram.Equiv`, the quotient by the equivalence relation
  generated by `Equiv`; it is the text's quotient as soon as `Equiv` is transitive.  Every
  invariant used (`[d]`, `|[D]|`, the push-forward of Theorem 10.5.4) is proved invariant under
  `Equiv`, hence well defined on these classes.
* **Definition 10.5.1.**  For the same reason the composition of generalized strong bundle maps is
  not constructed: `SEP(F⁻¹)` is formalized as a quiver (objects and morphisms), with the
  identities and the map `i : SEP → SEP(F⁻¹)`, `Ψ ↦ [W ←1 W →Ψ W']`, as a prefunctor.
* `|[D]|` is the map of orbit spaces of the diagram of total spaces
  `(W, 𝐖) ← (W'', 𝐖'') → (W', 𝐖')` (§10.3's `Diagram.orbitMap`), `|Φ|` being a homeomorphism by
  Definition 10.4.1 (1).
* `γ` is stated for any diagram of ep-groupoids; for `D` it is that of `D.base`.  The 2017 text has
  `g ↦ a ∘ g ∘ a⁻¹` in the first step; the 2021 `a⁻¹ ∘ g ∘ a` is used.
* Remark 10.5.3 is formalized as: a generalized strong bundle isomorphism covers a generalized
  isomorphism of the base ep-groupoids (Theorem 10.3.10), induces one of the total spaces, and
  `|[D]|` is a homeomorphism.

## Not formalized

* The equivariant germ `q` and its lift `Q` (10.25): local expressions which depend on the
  representative and on neighbourhoods with natural representations; the text uses no property
  of them.
* The composition in `SEP(F⁻¹)` and the transitivity of `Equiv` (the weak fibred product of strong
  bundles, see above), hence also the strong bundle version of Theorem 10.3.10 mentioned in
  Remark 10.5.3.
-/

